\subsection{Functions and variables (aspects 11--17)}

Seven aspects from the functions-and-variables analyses, one
question group each.

<<funcvars groups>>=
<<group 11: assignment is not an equation>>,
<<group 12: assignments are not re-evaluated>>,
<<group 13: defining does not run the body>>,
<<group 14: parameters bind from arguments, not names>>,
<<group 15: return hands back a value, not a name>>,
<<group 16: a list argument is shared, not copied>>,
<<group 17: a function must be defined before the call>>
@

\paragraph{Aspect 11: assignment is not an equation.}
The critical aspect is that \mintinline{python}{=} assigns --- computes
the right-hand side from the current values and stores the result ---
rather than states an equation
\autocite{Kohn2017VariableEvaluation,Plass2015Variables}.
The \enquote{no solution} distractor encodes the equation reading,
which the abstract of \textcite{Kohn2017VariableEvaluation} reports
verbatim as \enquote{believed to be outright impossible}; the
expression distractor encodes storing the calculation instead of its
value; the unchanged-value distractor encodes reading the second
assignment as inert.
Form B varies the name, the values and the correct position; the three
encoded misconceptions are the same.

