\subsection{Functions and lists, revisited (aspects 61--62)}

Two aspects from the debugging chapter's worked example: default
arguments are evaluated once, and mutation differs from rebinding.
The quiz pair is titled \enquote{functions and lists, revisited}
towards the students, since \enquote{debugging} would give away that
something is wrong in the programs.

<<debugging groups>>=
<<group 61: a default argument is evaluated once>>,
<<group 62: mutation is not rebinding>>
@

\paragraph{Aspect 61: a default argument is evaluated once.}
The critical aspect is that the default list is created once, at
definition time, and mutated by every call that uses the default ---
the surprising behaviour the debugging chapter's patterns of variation
isolate.
The fresh-default distractor encodes assuming a new list per call.

